rtain recent surgeries\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tDVT\n\t•\tImmobilization\n\t•\tSurgery\n\t•\tCancer\n\t•\tPregnancy\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tPrevious PE/DVT\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tMassive/high-risk PE\n\t2.\tIntermediate-risk PE\n\t3.\tLow-risk PE\n\nSurgical/Interventional Treatment\n\nFor selected severe cases:\n\t•\tCatheter-directed treatment\n\t•\tSurgical pulmonary embolectomy\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tCT pulmonary angiography\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tD-dimer\n\t•\tABG\n\t•\tEchocardiography\n\t•\tDoppler for DVT\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnticoagulation\n\t•\tOxygen if hypoxemic\n\t•\tThrombolysis for selected high-risk patients\n\t•\tCatheter/surgical treatment in selected severe cases\n\t•\tHemodynamic support\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRight heart failure\n\t•\tShock\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\t•\tRecurrent PE\n\t•\tDeath\n\t•\tChronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension\n\n⸻\n\n8. Kidney Transplant\n\nDefinition\n\nKidney transplantation is a surgery in which a healthy kidney from a donor is placed into a patient with end-stage kidney disease.\n\nEtiology/Indications\n\nKidney failure due to:\n\t•\tDiabetes mellitus\n\t•\tChronic glomerulonephritis\n\t•\tPolycystic kidney disease\n\t•\tChronic hypertension\n\t•\tOther causes of end-stage renal disease\n\nIndications\n\t•\tEnd-stage renal disease\n\t•\tSevere irreversible renal failure\n\t•\tPatient suitable for transplantation\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\t•\tActive untreated malignancy\n\t•\tSevere uncontrolled systemic disease\n\t•\tInability to safely undergo transplantation\n\nRisk Factors for Graft Failure\n\t•\tRejection\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tPoor medication adherence\n\t•\tCardiovascular disease\n\t•\tRecurrence of original disease\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tLiving donor transplant\n\t2.\tDeceased donor transplant\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tDonor kidney is removed.\n\t•\tKidney is placed usually in the lower abdomen/pelvis.\n\t•\tRenal artery and vein are connected to recipient vessels.\n\t•\tUreter is connected to the urinary bladder.\n\t•\tNative kidneys are usually left in place unless there is a specific reason to remove them.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tBlood group matching\n\t•\tHLA typing\n\t•\tCross-match\n\t•\tRenal function tests\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tInfection screening\n\t•\tImaging\n\nManagement\n\t•\tImmunosuppressive drugs\n\t•\tMonitor renal function\n\t•\tInfection prevention\n\t•\tBP and diabetes control\n\t•\tRegular follow-up\n\nComplications\n\t•\tAcute rejection\n\t•\tChronic rejection\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tVascular thrombosis\n\t•\tUrinary leakage\n\t•\tGraft failure\n\n⸻\n\n9. Portal Hypertension Management\n\nDefinition\n\nPortal hypertension is an increase in pressure within the portal venous system, commonly due to liver cirrhosis.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tLiver cirrhosis\n\t•\tPortal vein thrombosis\n\t•\tSchistosomiasis\n\t•\tHepatic vein obstruction\n\t•\tOther chronic liver diseases\n\nIndications for Intervention\n\t•\tVariceal bleeding\n\t•\tRecurrent bleeding\n\t•\tSevere portal hypertension\n\t•\tRefractory ascites\n\t•\tComplications not controlled by medical treatment\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on the procedure. Severe liver failure or severe cardiopulmonary disease may make some interventions unsuitable.\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChronic alcohol use\n\t•\tViral hepatitis\n\t•\tCirrhosis\n\t•\tPortal vein thrombosis\n\t•\tChronic liver disease\n\nTypes of Management\n\t1.\tMedical treatment\n\t2.\tEndoscopic treatment\n\t3.\tTIPS\n\t4.\tSurgical shunts\n\t5.\tLiver transplantation\n\nSurgical/Interventional Procedures\n\nEndoscopic variceal ligation\n\t•\tEnlarged esophageal veins are identified.\n\t•\tBands are placed around them to stop bleeding.\n\nTIPS – Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt\n\t•\tA channel is created between portal and hepatic veins.\n\t•\tA stent is placed.\n\t•\tPortal pressure is reduced.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tLiver function tests\n\t•\tUltrasound Doppler\n\t•\tCT/MRI\n\t•\tUpper GI endoscopy\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tCoagulation profile\n\nManagement\n\t•\tTreat underlying liver disease\n\t•\tBeta-blockers when indicated\n\t•\tEndoscopic variceal ligation\n\t•\tDiuretics for ascites\n\t•\tTIPS in selected patients\n\t•\tLiver transplantation in advanced disease\n\nComplications\n\t•\tEsophageal variceal bleeding\n\t•\tAscites\n\t•\tSplenomegaly\n\t•\tHepatic encephalopathy\n\t•\tLiver failure\n\t•\tDeath\n\n⸻\n\n10. Esophageal Surgery for Dysphagia\n\nDefinition\n\nDysphagia means difficulty in swallowing food or liquids.\n\nEsophageal surgery may be required when dysphagia is caused by structural obstruction or severe motility disorders.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tEsophageal cancer\n\t•\tAchalasia\n\t•\tEsophageal stricture\n\t•\tDiverticulum\n\t•\tSevere reflux-related narrowing\n\t•\tCongenital abnormalities\n\nIndications\n\t•\tSevere achalasia\n\t•\tEsophageal obstruction\n\t•\tCancer\n\t•\tSevere stricture\n\t•\tFailure of conservative treatment\n\nContraindications\n\nDepend on the specific operation:\n\t•\tSevere uncorrectable medical illness\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\t•\tPatient unfit for anesthesia\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tAlcohol\n\t•\tGERD\n\t•\tObesity\n\t•\tAchalasia\n\t•\tPrevious esophageal injury\n\nTypes of Surgery\n\t1.\tHeller myotomy – commonly for achalasia.\n\t2.\tPOEM – endoscopic muscle-cutting procedure for achalasia.\n\t3.\tEsophageal dilatation – for strictures.\n\t4.\tEsophagectomy – removal of part/all of the esophagus, usually for cancer.\n\t5.\tEsophageal reconstruction – restoring food passage after esophageal removal.\n\nSurgical Procedure – Heller Myotomy\n\t•\tSmall abdominal/chest incisions are made.\n\t•\tThe tight lower esophageal muscle is identified.\n\t•\tMuscle fibers are divided.\n\t•\tThe mucosa is preserved.\n\t•\tOften combined with an anti-reflux procedure.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tUpper GI endoscopy\n\t•\tBarium swallow\n\t•\tEsophageal manometry\n\t•\tCT scan\n\t•\tBiopsy when malignancy is suspected\n\nManagement\n\t•\tTreat underlying cause\n\t•\tDietary modification\n\t•\tEndoscopic dilatation when appropriate\n\t•\tSurgery for severe structural/motility disease\n\t•\tNutritional support\n\t•\tSwallowing rehabilitation\n\nComplications\n\t•\tEsophageal perforation\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tAspiration\n\t•\tGERD\n\t•\tAnastomotic leak after major reconstruction\n\t•\tRecurrent dysphagia\n\n⸻\n\n⭐ Quick Revision\n\nEmbolectomy → removal of embolus\nVascular reconstruction → repair/replacement/bypass of vessel\nAneurysm surgery → repair of dilated weak vessel\nAmputation → removal of non-viable limb\nGangrene surgery → removal of dead/infected tissue\nDVT → clot in deep vein\nPulmonary embolism → clot blocks pulmonary artery\nKidney transplant → replacement of failed kidney\nPortal hypertension → increased portal venous pressure\nEsophageal surgery → treatment of structural/motility causes of dysphagia\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-10T19:31:05+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:01:06Z","content":"Burns Plastic Surgery S Das BPT Exam Notes Easy language moderately explained Note These are simplified exam oriented notes based on standard surgical concepts covered in S Das They are paraphrased rather than copied verbatim --- **1 BURNS** **Definition** A burn is an injury to the skin and sometimes deeper tissues caused by heat chemicals electricity radiation or friction **Etiology** • Thermal flame hot liquid steam hot objects • Chemical acids and alkalis • Electrical electrical current • Radiation sunlight radiotherapy etc • Friction skin injury due to rubbing **Indications for Hospital / Surgical Management** • Deep or extensive burns • Burns involving face hands feet genitalia major joints • Circumferential burns • Electrical or chemical burns • Inhalation injury • Burns requiring skin grafting **Contraindications** There are no absolute contraindications to emergency burn treatment Definitive surgery may be delayed if the patient is haemodynamically unstable or medically unfit **Risk Factors** • Children and elderly people • Occupational exposure to fire / chemicals • Smoking and unsafe cooking practices • Poor electrical safety • Lack of protective equipment **Types** According to depth 1 Superficial burn epidermis involved red and painful 2 Partial thickness burn epidermis and part of dermis involved blistering and pain 3 Full thickness burn entire skin thickness destroyed skin may be white brown or charred and sensation may be reduced 4 Deep burn extends into deeper tissues such as fat muscle or bone According to cause thermal chemical electrical and radiation burns **Investigation** • Estimate percentage of total body surface area TBSA burned • Assess burn depth • Vital signs and urine output • CBC electrolytes renal function • ABG and carboxyhaemoglobin when inhalation injury is suspected • ECG in electrical burns • X ray / other imaging when associated injuries are suspected **Management** Immediate management ABCDE • A Airway assessment early intubation if significant inhalation injury is suspected • B Oxygen and breathing support • C IV access and fluid resuscitation for significant burns • D Neurological assessment and pain control • E Remove burning source and clothing / jewellery assess the whole body while preventing hypothermia Further management • Fluid and electrolyte replacement • Analgesics • Wound cleaning and appropriate dressings • Prevention / treatment of infection • Nutrition and adequate protein intake • Early excision and skin grafting for appropriate deep burns • Physiotherapy and splinting to prevent contractures **Complications** • Shock • Infection and sepsis • Inhalation injury • Electrolyte imbalance • Acute kidney injury • Scarring and contractures • Functional and cosmetic deformity • Psychological problems --- **2 RECONSTRUCTIVE SURGERY** **Definition** Reconstructive surgery restores form and function of a body part damaged by trauma burns congenital defects infection tumour or previous surgery **Etiology / Indications** Used for • Burn deformities • Traumatic tissue loss • Congenital deformities • Post tumour defects • Chronic wounds • Scar contractures **Contraindications** • Severe uncontrolled systemic illness • Active uncontrolled infection • Poor blood supply to the area • Patient not medically fit for anaesthesia / surgery **Risk Factors for Poor Reconstruction** • Smoking • Diabetes • Malnutrition • Infection • Poor vascular supply • Poor wound healing **Types** • Skin grafts • Local flaps • Regional flaps • Free flaps • Tissue expansion • Reconstructive microsurgery **Surgical Procedure** 1 Assess defect and surrounding tissue 2 Clean / debride unhealthy tissue 3 Select suitable reconstructive method 4 Transfer skin / tissue to cover the defect 5 Secure the graft / flap with sutures 6 Ensure adequate blood supply 7 Dress and monitor the reconstructed area **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Assessment of vascular supply • Blood tests • Doppler studies when required • Imaging for deeper or complex defects **Management** • Wound care • Infection control • Nutritional support • Appropriate reconstructive surgery • Physiotherapy and rehabilitation • Scar and contracture management **Complications** • Infection • Bleeding / haematoma • Poor wound healing • Graft or flap failure • Scar formation • Contracture • Functional or cosmetic deformity --- **3 SKIN GRAFTS** **Definition** A skin graft is a piece of skin completely separated from its original blood supply and transferred to another area to cover a wound **Indications** • Burns • Large wounds • Traumatic skin loss • Ulcers • Post tumour defects • Areas where primary closure is not possible **Contraindications** • Poorly vascularized wound bed • Active uncontrolled infection • Necrotic tissue • Patient medically unfit for surgery **Types** According to thickness 1 Split thickness skin graft STSG epidermis and part of dermis 2 Full thickness skin graft FTSG epidermis and entire dermis According to source • Autograft patients own skin • Allograft skin from another human • Xenograft skin from another species **Surgical Procedure** 1 Prepare and clean recipient wound 2 Harvest skin from a suitable donor site 3 Prepare graft and recipient bed 4 Place graft over wound 5 Secure with sutures / staples 6 Apply dressing and immobilize the area 7 Monitor graft take **Investigations** • Wound assessment • Vascularity of wound bed • CBC glucose and other routine preoperative investigations **Management** • Proper wound dressing • Immobilization initially • Monitor graft colour warmth and adherence • Treat infection if present • After healing moisturization pressure therapy and physiotherapy may be used **Complications** • Graft infection • Haematoma / seroma • Partial or complete graft loss • Poor graft take • Scar and contracture • Donor site infection or scarring --- **4 COSMETIC / AESTHETIC SURGERY** **Definition** Cosmetic surgery is surgery performed mainly to improve or alter physical appearance rather than to restore function after disease or injury **Indications** • Desired improvement in body or facial appearance • Correction of selected cosmetic deformities • Excess skin or localized fat • Changes associated with ageing **Contraindications** • Unrealistic expectations • Severe uncontrolled medical disease • Active infection • Poor psychological suitability • Inability to understand risks and postoperative care **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Obesity • Diabetes • Poor nutrition • Previous surgery / scarring • Poor wound healing tendency **Types** • Rhinoplasty • Blepharoplasty • Facelift • Liposuction • Abdominoplasty • Breast augmentation / reduction • Hair transplantation **Surgical Procedure** Depends on the procedure Generally Consultation examination planning anaesthesia surgical correction wound closure postoperative monitoring **Investigations** • Medical and surgical history • Physical examination • Routine blood investigations • ECG / imaging when indicated **Management** • Preoperative counselling • Appropriate surgical procedure • Pain and wound management • Prevention of infection • Gradual return to activity **Complications** • Bleeding • Infection • Anaesthetic complications • Scarring • Asymmetry • Nerve injury • Poor cosmetic result • Thromboembolic complications in selected operations --- **5 CONTRACTURE AND SCAR MANAGEMENT** **Definition** A scar is fibrous tissue formed during wound healing A contracture occurs when scar tissue shortens and restricts movement commonly after burns **Etiology** • Deep burns • Poorly healed wounds • Trauma • Infection • Surgery • Prolonged immobilization **Indications for Treatment** • Restricted joint movement • Pain or itching • Functional limitation • Significant cosmetic deformity • Hypertrophic or problematic scars **Contraindications** For conservative treatment there are few absolute contraindications Surgery may be delayed in active infection or when the patient is medically unfit **Risk Factors** • Deep burns • Large wounds • Healing by secondary intention • Infection • Poor rehabilitation • Scar crossing a joint **Types of Scars** • Normal scar • Hypertrophic scar • Keloid • Contracture scar **Investigation** Mainly clinical examination • Scar size thickness and mobility • Joint ROM • Functional assessment • Vascular / nerve status when required **Management** Conservative • Pressure garments • Silicone gel / sheets • Moisturization and massage • Stretching and ROM exercises • Splinting / positioning • Physiotherapy Surgical • Scar release • Z plasty • Skin grafting • Local / regional / free flap reconstruction when required **Complications** • Restricted ROM • Deformity • Pain and itching • Recurrent contracture • Functional disability • Psychological / cosmetic problems --- **6 MICROVASCULAR / MICROSURGICAL PROCEDURES** **Definition** Microsurgery is surgery performed using magnification and fine instruments to repair or join very small blood vessels and nerves **Indications** • Free flap reconstruction • Replantation of amputated parts • Complex traumatic defects • Head and neck reconstruction • Breast reconstruction • Limb reconstruction **Contraindications** • Patient medically unfit • Severe uncontrolled infection • Inadequate recipient vessels • Severe peripheral vascular disease in selected cases **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Diabetes • Vascular disease • Infection • Poor nutrition • Excessive tension on the reconstructed vessels **Types** • Free tissue transfer / free flap • Microvascular arterial repair • Microvascular venous repair • Nerve repair • Replantation **Surgical Procedure** 1 Prepare recipient and donor sites 2 Harvest tissue with its artery and vein 3 Transfer tissue to the defect 4 Under microscope artery and vein are joined anastomosis 5 Blood flow is restored 6 Flap is monitored for adequate circulation 7 Wound is closed and postoperative monitoring is continued **Investigations** • Clinical vascular examination • Doppler / Doppler ultrasound • CT angiography or other vascular imaging when needed • Routine preoperative blood investigations **Management** • Maintain adequate circulation and oxygenation • Keep the reconstructed limb / area appropriately positioned • Frequent flap monitoring • Avoid compression of the vascular pedicle • Early detection of vascular compromise • Rehabilitation after stabilization **Complications** • Arterial or venous thrombosis • Flap congestion • Flap ischemia and necrosis • Infection • Bleeding / haematoma • Anastomotic failure • Nerve dysfunction • Partial or complete flap loss --- **Quick Revision** Burns ABCDE fluids wound care infection prevention excision / grafting physiotherapy Reconstructive surgery Defect assessment debridement graft / flap wound closure rehabilitation Skin graft Harvest skin prepare wound place graft secure dressing monitor graft take Contracture Stretching splinting pressure / silicone scar care surgical release if required Microsurgery Harvest tissue transfer microscopic vessel anastomosis restore blood flow monitor flap //////////////////////////////////////////// Absolutely Below are ENT notes in the same order as your cardiac surgery answer Definition Etiology Indications Contraindications Risk factors Types Surgical procedure Investigations Management Complications in easy exam oriented language suitable for BPT university exams and broadly aligned with S Das surgical approach ENT EAR NOSE AND THROAT --- **1 ANATOMY AND PHYSIOLOGY OF HEARING** **Definition** Hearing is the ability to perceive sound through the ear and interpret it by the brain **Anatomy of Ear** The ear is divided into 3 parts A External ear • Pinna / auricle • External auditory canal • Tympanic membrane Function Collects and conducts sound waves toward the middle ear B Middle ear Contains • Tympanic cavity • Malleus • Incus • Stapes • Eustachian tube Function Transmits and amplifies sound vibrations from tympanic membrane to the inner ear C Inner ear Contains • Cochlea • Vestibule • Semicircular canals Cochlea hearing Vestibule semicircular canals balance **Physiology of Hearing** Sound pathway Sound waves Pinna External auditory canal Tympanic membrane Malleus Incus Stapes Oval window Cochlea Organ of Corti Auditory nerve Brain Mechanism 1 Pinna collects sound 2 Sound travels through external auditory canal 3 Tympanic membrane vibrates 4 Ossicles transmit and amplify vibration 5 Stapes moves at oval window 6 Fluid movement occurs inside cochlea 7 Hair cells of Organ of Corti are stimulated 8 Mechanical energy becomes electrical nerve impulses 9 Impulses travel through CN VIII vestibulocochlear nerve 10 Brain interprets the impulses as sound --- **2 AUDIOMETRY** **Definition** Audiometry is a method of measuring a persons hearing ability using different sound frequencies and intensities **Indications** • Hearing loss • Deafness • Tinnitus • Vertigo • Suspected ear disease • Pre operative assessment • Occupational hearing assessment • Hearing aid selection **Types** 1 Pure Tone Audiometry PTA Measures hearing thresholds for different frequencies 2 Speech Audiometry Measures • Speech detection • Speech recognition / discrimination 3 Impedance Audiometry Includes • Tympanometry • Acoustic reflex testing 4 Bone conduction audiometry Tests sound conduction through skull bones 5 Air conduction audiometry Tests the complete hearing pathway through the external and middle ear **Procedure** • Patient sits in a quiet / soundproof room • Headphones are placed over the ears • Different frequencies and intensities of sound are given • Patient indicates when the sound is heard • Hearing threshold is recorded **Interpretation** Conductive hearing loss Problem in • External ear • Middle ear Air conduction is poorer than bone conduction Sensorineural hearing loss Problem in • Cochlea • Auditory nerve Air and bone conduction are both reduced Mixed hearing loss Combination of • Conductive loss • Sensorineural loss **Management** Depends on cause • Medical treatment • Surgery • Hearing aid • Cochlear implant • Communication training --- **3 OTITIS MEDIA** **Definition** Otitis media is inflammation or infection of the middle ear **Etiology** • Bacterial infection • Viral infection • Upper respiratory tract infection • Eustachian tube dysfunction • Allergy • Adenoid enlargement Common organisms include • Streptococcus pneumoniae • Haemophilus influenzae • Moraxella catarrhalis **Indications for Surgery** Surgery may be required in • Recurrent otitis media • Persistent middle ear fluid • Chronic suppurative otitis media • Cholesteatoma • Complications **Contraindications** There is no absolute contraindication to necessary surgery Elective surgery may be postponed with • Acute uncontrolled infection • Severe medical illness • Unfit for anaesthesia **Risk Factors** • Children • Recurrent URTI • Day care exposure • Passive smoking • Bottle feeding while lying down • Adenoid hypertrophy • Eustachian tube abnormalities **Types** 1 Acute otitis media Sudden infection and inflammation 2 Otitis media with effusion Fluid is present without acute infection 3 Chronic suppurative otitis media Persistent / recurrent infection with ear discharge and perforation **Clinical Features** • Ear pain • Fever • Hearing loss • Ear discharge • Irritability in children • Feeling of blocked ear • Tinnitus **Investigations** • Otoscopy • Audiometry • Tympanometry • CT temporal bone in complicated disease • Culture of discharge when required **Management** • Analgesics • Appropriate antibiotics when bacterial infection is suspected • Treat nasal / upper airway problems • Myringotomy • Grommet / tympanostomy tube • Mastoid surgery for selected chronic disease **Complications** • Hearing loss • Tympanic membrane perforation • Mastoiditis • Cholesteatoma • Facial nerve palsy • Labyrinthitis • Meningitis • Brain abscess --- **4 SINUSITIS** **Definition** Sinusitis is inflammation of the mucosa of one or more paranasal sinuses **Etiology** • Viral infection • Bacterial infection • Allergy • Nasal polyps • Deviated nasal septum • Dental infection • Poor sinus drainage **Indications for Surgery** • Chronic sinusitis despite medical treatment • Nasal polyps • Obstructed sinus drainage • Recurrent sinusitis • Complications • Anatomical obstruction **Contraindications** Relative • Uncontrolled systemic disease • Active severe infection requiring stabilization • Unfit for anaesthesia **Risk Factors** • Allergy • Smoking • Nasal obstruction • Deviated septum • Nasal polyps • Recurrent URTI • Immunodeficiency **Types** According to duration 1 Acute sinusitis 2 Subacute sinusitis 3 Chronic sinusitis 4 Recurrent acute sinusitis According to sinus involved • Maxillary • Frontal • Ethmoidal • Sphenoidal • Pansinusitis **Clinical Features** • Nasal blockage • Facial pain / pressure • Headache • Nasal discharge • Postnasal drip • Reduced smell • Fever • Cough **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Nasal endoscopy • X ray in selected cases • CT scan of paranasal sinuses • Allergy testing when indicated **Management** • Saline nasal irrigation • Analgesics • Intranasal corticosteroids • Antibiotics when bacterial infection is suspected • Treat allergy • Endoscopic sinus surgery when indicated **Complications** • Orbital cellulitis • Orbital abscess • Osteomyelitis • Meningitis • Brain abscess • Cavernous sinus thrombosis --- **5 MASTOID SURGERY** **Definition** Mastoid surgery mastoidectomy is an operation in which infected or diseased mastoid air cells are removed **Etiology / Indications** Usually performed because of • Chronic suppurative otitis media • Cholesteatoma • Mastoiditis • Persistent ear infection • Complications of middle ear disease **Types** 1 Simple / cortical mastoidectomy Diseased mastoid air cells are removed while preserving important structures 2 Modified radical mastoidectomy Disease is removed while attempting to preserve useful hearing structures when possible 3 Radical mastoidectomy More extensive removal of diseased tissue used for extensive disease **Contraindications** • Patient medically unfit for anaesthesia • Uncontrolled systemic illness • Acute conditions requiring stabilization **Risk Factors for Mastoid Disease** • Chronic otitis media • Recurrent ear infection • Cholesteatoma • Poor Eustachian tube function **Investigations** • Otoscopy • Audiometry • Tympanometry • CT temporal bone • Blood tests • Culture of discharge **Surgical Procedure** 1 General anaesthesia 2 Incision is made behind the ear 3 Mastoid bone is exposed 4 Diseased mastoid air cells are drilled out 5 Infection / cholesteatoma is removed 6 Important structures are preserved where possible 7 Wound is closed and dressed **Management** Post operative • Antibiotics when indicated • Analgesics • Wound care • Keep ear dry • Follow up examination • Audiological assessment • Gradual return to activity **Complications** • Facial nerve injury • Hearing loss • Dizziness / vertigo • CSF leak • Infection • Bleeding • Taste disturbance • Recurrence of disease --- **6 TRACHEOSTOMY** **Definition** Tracheostomy is a surgical procedure in which an opening is made in the anterior wall of the trachea and a tube is inserted to maintain an airway **Indications** A Upper airway obstruction • Tumor • Trauma • Foreign body • Severe edema B Prolonged mechanical ventilation C Airway protection • Neurological disorders • Reduced consciousness • Poor cough D Removal of secretions When the patient cannot clear secretions effectively **Contraindications** There are few absolute contraindications in an emergency Relative • Uncorrected bleeding disorder • Severe anatomical abnormality • Local infection • Unstable patient **Risk Factors** • Bleeding disorders • Obesity • Previous neck surgery • Neck tumors • Anatomical abnormalities • Emergency procedure **Types** According to duration • Temporary • Permanent According to site • Upper tracheostomy • Middle tracheostomy • Lower tracheostomy **Surgical Procedure** 1 Patient is positioned appropriately 2 Anaesthesia is given 3 Neck is exposed and incision is made 4 Trachea is identified 5 Opening is made in tracheal rings 6 Tracheostomy tube is inserted 7 Tube is secured 8 Oxygen / ventilation is connected as required **Investigations** • Airway examination • Oxygen saturation • ABG • Chest X ray when indicated • CBC • Coagulation profile **Management** Tracheostomy care • Maintain tube patency • Suction secretions when required • Humidification • Stoma care • Cuff pressure monitoring when cuffed tube is used • Oxygen therapy • Chest physiotherapy • Communication support • Swallowing assessment Physiotherapy • Positioning • Breathing exercises • Chest expansion • Airway clearance • Suctioning when trained / indicated • Early mobilization **Complications** Early • Bleeding • Pneumothorax • Tube displacement • Airway obstruction • Infection • Subcutaneous emphysema Late • Tracheal stenosis • Tracheoesophageal fistula • Granulation tissue • Difficulty decannulation • Scar formation --- **7 HEARING AIDS** **Definition** A hearing aid is an electronic device that amplifies and modifies sound to help a person with hearing loss hear better **Indications** • Mild to severe hearing loss • Conductive hearing loss when surgery is unsuitable • Sensorineural hearing loss • Mixed hearing loss • Age related hearing loss **Contraindications / Limitations** • Untreated active ear infection • Severe ear discharge until treated • Profound hearing loss where hearing aid provides inadequate benefit • Patient unable to use / manage the device **Types** 1 Behind the ear BTE Device sits behind the ear 2 In the ear ITE Fits within the outer ear 3 In the canal ITC Fits partly inside ear canal 4 Completely in canal CIC Fits almost completely inside canal **Components** • Microphone • Amplifier / processor • Receiver / speaker • Battery / power source **Working** Sound microphone amplifier / processor receiver ear **Investigations before fitting** • Otoscopy • Pure tone audiometry • Speech audiometry • Hearing loss assessment • Ear mould assessment when required **Management** • Select appropriate hearing aid • Proper fitting • Programming • Patient education • Regular cleaning • Battery / device care • Follow up hearing assessment **Complications / Problems** • Ear irritation • Feedback / whistling • Poor sound quality • Improper fitting • Ear blockage sensation • Poor compliance --- **8 FACIAL NERVE PALSY SURGERIES** **Definition** Facial nerve palsy is weakness or paralysis of the muscles supplied by the facial nerve CN VII **Etiology** • Bells palsy • Trauma • Temporal bone fracture • Tumor • Ear disease • Mastoid surgery • Parotid surgery • Infections • Congenital causes **Indications for Surgery** Surgery may be considered in • Severe nerve injury • Complete paralysis with poor recovery • Traumatic nerve transection • Persistent paralysis • Facial deformity • Eye protection problems • Long standing paralysis **Types of Surgery** • Facial nerve decompression in selected cases • Direct nerve repair • Nerve grafting • Nerve transfer • Muscle transfer • Static facial suspension • Eyelid procedures for corneal protection **Contraindications** • Severe uncontrolled systemic illness • Patient unfit for anaesthesia • Poor surgical prognosis in selected cases **Risk Factors** Depend on cause • Head trauma • Ear surgery • Tumors • Severe ear infections • Temporal bone fracture **Investigations** • Complete facial nerve examination • Audiometry • Electroneurography • Electromyography • CT temporal bone • MRI brain / internal auditory canal when indicated **Surgical Procedure** Depends on the cause 1 Identify damaged nerve 2 Remove compression if present 3 Repair nerve directly if possible 4 Use nerve graft if there is a gap 5 Nerve transfer or muscle transfer may be used in chronic cases 6 Eye protection surgery may be performed when required **Management** • Protect the eye • Lubricating eye drops • Eye patch / taping when indicated • Facial exercises • Neuromuscular retraining • Massage where appropriate • Speech / swallowing therapy if affected • Surgical correction when required **Complications** • Persistent facial weakness • Incomplete eye closure • Corneal injury • Synkinesis • Facial asymmetry • Difficulty eating / speaking • Psychological problems --- **9 VERTIGO** **Definition** Vertigo is a false sensation of movement usually feeling that the person or surroundings are spinning **Etiology** Peripheral causes • Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo BPPV • Meniere disease • Vestibular neuritis • Labyrinthitis • Otitis media Central causes • Stroke • Brain tumor • Multiple sclerosis • Cerebellar disease • Migraine **Indications for Surgery** Surgery is reserved for selected severe conditions such as • Persistent Meniere disease • Certain tumors • Severe structural disease • Vertigo not controlled by conservative treatment **Contraindications** Depend on the proposed surgery and patients medical fitness **Risk Factors** • Increasing age • Previous ear disease • Head trauma • Migraine • Vascular disease • Certain medications **Types** Peripheral vertigo Originates from the inner ear / vestibular nerve Central vertigo Originates from the brainstem or cerebellum **Clinical Features** • Spinning sensation • Nausea / vomiting • Loss of balance • Nystagmus • Sweating • Difficulty walking • Hearing symptoms in some peripheral disorders **Investigations** • Vestibular examination • Dix Hallpike test • Head impulse test • Audiometry • Tympanometry when appropriate • VNG / ENG • MRI brain when central cause is suspected • CT when indicated **Management** BPPV • Canalith repositioning manoeuvre e g Epley manoeuvre • Vestibular exercises Other causes • Treat underlying disease • Vestibular rehabilitation • Medicines for selected patients • Surgery in selected severe cases Physiotherapy • Vestibular adaptation • Gaze stabilization • Balance training • Habituation exercises • Gait training • Fall prevention **Complications** • Falls • Injury • Anxiety • Reduced mobility • Difficulty performing ADLs • Persistent imbalance --- **10 OTOSCLEROSIS** **Definition** Otosclerosis is an abnormal fixation / remodelling of the bones of the middle ear especially the stapes causing progressive conductive hearing loss **Etiology** • Abnormal bone remodeling • Genetic / familial factors • Sometimes associated with hormonal influences **Indications for Surgery** • Significant conductive hearing loss • Progressive hearing loss • Patient wants improved hearing • Good surgical candidate **Contraindications** • Active ear infection • Severe medical illness • Poor surgical fitness **Risk Factors** • Family history • Female sex • Genetic predisposition • Pregnancy may influence progression in some patients **Types** • Fenestral otosclerosis • Cochlear / retrofenestral otosclerosis **Clinical Features** • Progressive hearing loss • Usually conductive initially • Tinnitus • Usually no ear discharge • Vertigo may occur in some cases **Investigations** • Otoscopy • Pure tone audiometry • Speech audiometry • Tympanometry • CT temporal bone in selected cases **Surgical Procedure** Stapedectomy / Stapedotomy 1 Middle ear is accessed 2 Fixed stapes is treated 3 A small opening is made in the footplate in stapedotomy 4 Prosthesis is inserted to transmit sound to the inner ear **Management** • Observation in mild cases • Hearing aid • Stapedotomy / stapedectomy when indicated • Regular audiological follow up **Complications** • Sensorineural hearing loss • Vertigo • Tinnitus • Taste disturbance • Facial nerve injury • Prosthesis displacement • Persistent hearing loss --- **11 APHONIA** **Definition** Aphonia is complete loss of the ability to produce a normal voice **Etiology** • Laryngeal disease • Vocal cord paralysis • Laryngeal tumor • Severe inflammation • Trauma • Neurological disorders • Functional / psychogenic causes • Excessive voice use **Indications for Surgery** Surgery may be required for • Vocal cord paralysis • Tumor • Structural vocal cord lesion • Severe airway / voice abnormality **Contraindications** Depend on the planned operation and general medical fitness **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Excessive voice use • Laryngeal infection • Vocal cord trauma • Neck / chest surgery • Neurological disease **Types** • Organic aphonia • Functional / psychogenic aphonia **Investigations** • Laryngoscopy • Flexible nasolaryngoscopy • Stroboscopy • Imaging when indicated • Neurological examination • Voice assessment **Management** • Voice rest • Treatment of underlying disease • Speech / voice therapy • Vocal hygiene • Surgery when indicated • Psychological management for functional aphonia **Complications** • Persistent voice loss • Communication difficulty • Social / occupational problems • Aspiration in associated vocal cord paralysis • Psychological distress --- **12 DEAFNESS** **Definition** Deafness is partial or complete loss of the ability to hear sounds **Etiology** Conductive causes • Wax • Otitis media • Tympanic membrane perforation • Ossicular problems • Otosclerosis Sensorineural causes • Cochlear damage • Noise exposure • Aging • Ototoxic drugs • Genetic causes • Viral infections • Auditory nerve disorders Mixed Combination of conductive and sensorineural hearing loss **Indications for Surgery** Depending on cause • Chronic ear disease • Otosclerosis • Cholesteatoma • Ossicular damage • Severe conductive loss • Cochlear implantation for selected severe / profound sensorineural loss **Contraindications** Depend on procedure Examples • Active infection for elective implant surgery • Severe medical illness • Anatomical limitations • Inability to undergo anaesthesia **Risk Factors** • Noise exposure • Increasing age • Ear infections • Ototoxic medications • Head trauma • Genetic factors • Congenital infections • Prematurity and certain neonatal complications **Types** 1 Conductive hearing loss Problem in external or middle ear 2 Sensorineural hearing loss Problem in inner ear or auditory nerve 3 Mixed hearing loss Both conductive and sensorineural components **Investigations** • Otoscopy • Pure tone audiometry • Speech audiometry • Tympanometry • Bone conduction testing • Otoacoustic emissions • Auditory brainstem response • CT / MRI when indicated **Management** Conductive • Remove wax • Treat infection • Tympanoplasty • Ossicular surgery • Stapedotomy when appropriate Sensorineural • Hearing aid • Cochlear implant in selected severe / profound cases • Communication training • Treatment of underlying cause Rehabilitation • Auditory training • Speech therapy • Lip reading / sign language support when appropriate • Assistive listening devices **Complications** • Communication difficulties • Delayed speech / language development in children • Social isolation • Educational difficulties • Reduced quality of life • Balance problems in associated vestibular disease //////////////////// Sure Below are Circulatory Surgeries in the same order with an easy exam oriented format definition etiology indications contraindications risk factors types surgical procedure investigations management complications Circulatory Surgeries Related Conditions --- **1 Embolectomy** **Definition** Embolectomy is a surgical procedure to remove an embolus blood clot or other material blocking a blood vessel and restore blood flow **Etiology** • Blood clot from heart or large vessels • Atrial fibrillation • Recent surgery • Deep vein thrombosis • Trauma to blood vessels • Prosthetic heart valves **Indications** • Acute arterial blockage • Severe limb ischemia • Sudden loss of circulation • Threatened limb • Pulmonary embolism in selected severe cases **Contraindications** • Severe irreversible ischemic damage • Very small / distal vessel obstruction unsuitable for removal • Patient unfit for surgery **Risk Factors** • Atherosclerosis • Atrial fibrillation • DVT • Immobilization • Recent surgery • Hypercoagulable states • Smoking **Types** 1 Arterial embolectomy removal of embolus from an artery 2 Pulmonary embolectomy removal of clot from pulmonary arteries 3 Catheter embolectomy clot removed using a balloon catheter **Surgical Procedure** • Vessel is exposed • Vessel is opened • A catheter may be passed beyond the clot • Balloon is inflated and withdrawn • Clot is removed • Blood flow is checked • Vessel is closed **Investigations** • Doppler ultrasound • CT angiography • MR angiography • ECG • Blood coagulation tests **Management** • Anticoagulants • Pain control • Treatment of underlying cause • Limb elevation when appropriate • Early mobilization after stabilization **Complications** • Re embolism • Bleeding • Vessel injury • Infection • Tissue ischemia • Compartment syndrome --- **2 Vascular Reconstruction** **Definition** Vascular reconstruction is surgery performed to repair replace or bypass a damaged or blocked blood vessel to restore normal circulation **Etiology** • Atherosclerosis • Trauma • Aneurysm • Congenital vascular abnormalities • Thrombosis **Indications** • Severe arterial blockage • Traumatic vessel injury • Aneurysm • Critical limb ischemia • Vascular occlusion **Contraindications** • Severe infection at operative site • Very poor general condition • Irreversible tissue damage • Unsuitable distal vessels **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Diabetes • Hypertension • Hyperlipidemia • Obesity • Older age **Types** 1 Bypass grafting 2 Endarterectomy 3 Vascular graft / interposition 4 Patch angioplasty 5 Angioplasty and stenting **Surgical Procedure** • Blocked / damaged vessel is identified • A graft may be taken from the patient or an artificial graft may be used • Blood is diverted around the obstruction • Graft is connected above and below the blockage • Blood flow is checked **Investigations** • Doppler • CT angiography • Conventional angiography • ABI • Blood tests **Management** • Antiplatelet / anticoagulant therapy • Control diabetes and BP • Smoking cessation • Wound care • Exercise rehabilitation **Complications** • Graft thrombosis • Infection • Bleeding • Re occlusion • Poor wound healing • Limb ischemia --- **3 Aneurysm Surgery** **Definition** An aneurysm is an abnormal localized dilatation or ballooning of a blood vessel due to weakness of its wall **Etiology** • Atherosclerosis • Hypertension • Congenital weakness • Trauma • Infection **Indications for Surgery** • Large aneurysm • Rapidly increasing size • Symptomatic aneurysm • Risk of rupture • Ruptured aneurysm **Contraindications** Usually relative and depend on the patients surgical risk • Severe cardiac disease • Severe respiratory disease • Uncontrolled infection • Very poor general condition **Risk Factors** • Hypertension • Smoking • Atherosclerosis • Older age • Family history • Male sex **Types** • Abdominal aortic aneurysm AAA • Thoracic aortic aneurysm • Peripheral artery aneurysm • Cerebral aneurysm **Surgical Procedure** Two common approaches 1 Open repair • Aneurysm is exposed • Blood flow is controlled • Affected segment is replaced with a graft 2 Endovascular repair • Catheter is introduced through an artery • A stent graft is placed inside the vessel • Blood flows through the graft instead of the aneurysm **Investigations** • Ultrasound • CT angiography • MRI • Angiography **Management** • BP control • Smoking cessation • Regular monitoring for small aneurysms • Surgical / endovascular repair when indicated **Complications** • Rupture • Severe internal bleeding • Thrombosis • Organ ischemia • Infection • Death --- **4 Amputation** **Definition** Amputation is the surgical removal of a part or whole limb when it cannot be preserved or is dangerous to the patient **Etiology** • Severe peripheral vascular disease • Gangrene • Diabetes • Severe trauma • Malignancy • Severe infection **Indications** • Irreversible gangrene • Non healing infected wound • Severe ischemia • Severe trauma • Malignant tumor • Life threatening infection **Contraindications** There are generally no absolute contraindications when amputation is life saving but surgery may be delayed for • Uncontrolled shock • Severe infection requiring stabilization • Poor general condition **Risk Factors** • Diabetes • Peripheral arterial disease • Smoking • Infection • Poor wound healing • Trauma **Types** According to level • Toe amputation • Transmetatarsal • Below knee amputation • Above knee amputation • Hip disarticulation **Surgical Procedure** • Diseased tissue is removed • Healthy tissue is preserved as much as possible • Bone is cut at an appropriate level • Muscles are balanced and attached • Nerves are handled carefully • Skin is closed to form a stump **Investigations** • Doppler • Angiography • X ray • Blood sugar • CBC • Infection markers **Management** • Pain control • Wound care • Prevention of contractures • Stump care • Strengthening exercises • Balance and gait training • Prosthetic training **Complications** • Phantom limb sensation / pain • Infection • Bleeding • Stump pain • Joint contracture • Poor wound healing • Psychological problems --- **5 Gangrene Surgery** **Definition** Gangrene is the death of body tissue due to loss of blood supply infection or both **Etiology** • Severe arterial obstruction • Diabetes • Infection • Trauma • Frostbite • Severe burns **Indications for Surgery** • Dead tissue • Spreading infection • Wet gangrene • Sepsis • Severe irreversible ischemia **Contraindications** Surgery may be temporarily delayed until the patient is stabilized in • Severe shock • Uncontrolled metabolic abnormalities **Risk Factors** • Diabetes • Peripheral vascular disease • Smoking • Infection • Trauma • Immunosuppression **Types** 1 Dry gangrene mainly due to loss of blood supply 2 Wet gangrene dead tissue with infection 3 Gas gangrene caused by gas producing bacteria **Surgical Procedure** Depending on severity • Debridement removal of dead tissue • Drainage of infection • Revascularization • Amputation if tissue cannot be saved **Investigations** • Doppler • X ray • CT / MRI when required • Blood culture • CBC • Blood glucose **Management** • Antibiotics for infection • Revascularization when possible • Debridement • Blood sugar control • Amputation when necessary **Complications** • Sepsis • Septic shock • Tissue loss • Amputation • Multi organ failure • Death --- **6 Deep Vein Thrombosis DVT** **Definition** DVT is the formation of a blood clot inside a deep vein usually in the leg **Etiology** DVT commonly occurs due to Virchows triad 1 Venous stasis 2 Vessel wall injury 3 Increased blood clotting **Indications for Treatment** • Confirmed DVT • Prevention of clot extension • Prevention of pulmonary embolism **Contraindications to Anticoagulation** • Active major bleeding • Severe bleeding disorder • Recent certain types of major surgery • Severe thrombocytopenia **Risk Factors** • Prolonged bed rest • Long distance travel • Surgery • Pregnancy • Cancer • Obesity • Previous DVT • Immobilization **Types** • Proximal DVT femoral / iliac veins • Distal DVT calf veins • Upper limb DVT **Surgical / Interventional Treatment** Usually DVT is treated medically rather than by routine surgery In severe selected cases • Catheter directed thrombolysis • Thrombectomy • Inferior vena cava filter when anticoagulation cannot be used and PE risk is significant **Investigations** • Duplex Doppler ultrasound • D dimer • CT / MR venography in selected cases **Management** • Anticoagulants • Compression therapy when appropriate • Early mobilization after adequate treatment • Treat underlying cause • Prevention of recurrence **Complications** • Pulmonary embolism • Post thrombotic syndrome • Chronic venous insufficiency • Recurrent DVT --- **7 Pulmonary Embolism PE** **Definition** Pulmonary embolism occurs when a blood clot blocks a pulmonary artery usually 