nor kidney is removed • Kidney is placed usually in the lower abdomen / pelvis • Renal artery and vein are connected to recipient vessels • Ureter is connected to the urinary bladder • Native kidneys are usually left in place unless there is a specific reason to remove them **Investigations** • Blood group matching • HLA typing • Cross match • Renal function tests • CBC • Infection screening • Imaging **Management** • Immunosuppressive drugs • Monitor renal function • Infection prevention • BP and diabetes control • Regular follow up **Complications** • Acute rejection • Chronic rejection • Infection • Bleeding • Vascular thrombosis • Urinary leakage • Graft failure --- **9 Portal Hypertension Management** **Definition** Portal hypertension is an increase in pressure within the portal venous system commonly due to liver cirrhosis **Etiology** • Liver cirrhosis • Portal vein thrombosis • Schistosomiasis • Hepatic vein obstruction • Other chronic liver diseases **Indications for Intervention** • Variceal bleeding • Recurrent bleeding • Severe portal hypertension • Refractory ascites • Complications not controlled by medical treatment **Contraindications** Depend on the procedure Severe liver failure or severe cardiopulmonary disease may make some interventions unsuitable **Risk Factors** • Chronic alcohol use • Viral hepatitis • Cirrhosis • Portal vein thrombosis • Chronic liver disease **Types of Management** 1 Medical treatment 2 Endoscopic treatment 3 TIPS 4 Surgical shunts 5 Liver transplantation **Surgical / Interventional Procedures** Endoscopic variceal ligation • Enlarged esophageal veins are identified • Bands are placed around them to stop bleeding TIPS Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt • A channel is created between portal and hepatic veins • A stent is placed • Portal pressure is reduced **Investigations** • Liver function tests • Ultrasound Doppler • CT / MRI • Upper GI endoscopy • CBC • Coagulation profile **Management** • Treat underlying liver disease • Beta blockers when indicated • Endoscopic variceal ligation • Diuretics for ascites • TIPS in selected patients • Liver transplantation in advanced disease **Complications** • Esophageal variceal bleeding • Ascites • Splenomegaly • Hepatic encephalopathy • Liver failure • Death --- **10 Esophageal Surgery for Dysphagia** **Definition** Dysphagia means difficulty in swallowing food or liquids Esophageal surgery may be required when dysphagia is caused by structural obstruction or severe motility disorders **Etiology** • Esophageal cancer • Achalasia • Esophageal stricture • Diverticulum • Severe reflux related narrowing • Congenital abnormalities **Indications** • Severe achalasia • Esophageal obstruction • Cancer • Severe stricture • Failure of conservative treatment **Contraindications** Depend on the specific operation • Severe uncorrectable medical illness • Active uncontrolled infection • Patient unfit for anesthesia **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Alcohol • GERD • Obesity • Achalasia • Previous esophageal injury **Types of Surgery** 1 Heller myotomy commonly for achalasia 2 POEM endoscopic muscle cutting procedure for achalasia 3 Esophageal dilatation for strictures 4 Esophagectomy removal of part / all of the esophagus usually for cancer 5 Esophageal reconstruction restoring food passage after esophageal removal **Surgical Procedure Heller Myotomy** • Small abdominal / chest incisions are made • The tight lower esophageal muscle is identified • Muscle fibers are divided • The mucosa is preserved • Often combined with an anti reflux procedure **Investigations** • Upper GI endoscopy • Barium swallow • Esophageal manometry • CT scan • Biopsy when malignancy is suspected **Management** • Treat underlying cause • Dietary modification • Endoscopic dilatation when appropriate • Surgery for severe structural / motility disease • Nutritional support • Swallowing rehabilitation **Complications** • Esophageal perforation • Bleeding • Infection • Aspiration • G