y section by adding risk factors like atherosclerosis and atrial fibrillation, following the structured format. I'm focusing on defining the types, the surgical procedures, the investigations, the management, and the associated complications. This should give a complete view of Embolectomy for surgical study.\n\n\n**Defining Embolectomy Types**\n\nI am now categorizing the types of embolectomy procedures. I'm focusing on the types, surgical procedures, the required investigations, post-operative management, and any related complications, following the structured format. I am now providing a comprehensive overview.\n\n\n**Defining Embolectomy Procedures**\n\nI am now structuring the surgical procedure section for embolectomy, covering vessel identification, graft selection (autologous vs. synthetic), and the creation of proximal and distal anastomoses to bypass the obstruction. I'm noting the need to check blood flow and any associated techniques. I am also detailing the essential post-operative management steps, and the required investigations.\n\n\n**Defining Aneurysm Surgery**\n\nI'm now focusing on defining aneurysm surgery, including key complications like graft thrombosis and poor wound healing. I am detailing definitions, etiologies, indications, contraindications, and risk factors associated with this surgical intervention, alongside the available types. I'm focusing on providing a well-structured and comprehensive overview.\n\n\n**Defining Aneurysm Surgery**\n\nI'm now detailing the surgical procedures and management of aneurysms. I am listing the two common approaches, namely open and endovascular repair. Additionally, I am listing the required investigations and management strategies to provide a comprehensive overview. I have also added complications, like rupture and thrombosis.\n\n\n**Defining Amputation Procedures**\n\nI'm now detailing amputation procedures. I'm focusing on the types, from toe to hip disarticulation, and related contraindications. I'm detailing the surgical procedure and subsequent investigations and management, including pain control, wound care, and stump care. My priority is providing a practical and concise overview.\n\n\n**Summarizing Gangrene Procedures**\n\nI'm now summarizing gangrene, adding key surgical details. I'm focusing on the types (dry, wet, gas), and surgical procedures like debridement, drainage, revascularization, and amputation. I'm structuring this to cover all necessary aspects, including etiology, indications, and contraindications.\n\n\n**Defining DVT Management**\n\nI'm now detailing DVT, starting with definitions, and covering etiology, and indications. I'm building on this foundation to describe contraindications to anticoagulation and associated risk factors. I am building a structure, and detailing investigations and management strategies. The next step will be covering the surgical procedures and management.\n\n\n**Expanding DVT Management**\n\nI am now expanding on DVT management to include specific intervention treatments. I am outlining surgical and interventional treatments, including catheter-directed thrombolysis, thrombectomy, and inferior vena cava filters. I am now detailing the investigations required, which are Duplex Doppler ultrasound, D-dimer, and CT/MR venography. Finally, I'm noting the management strategies: anticoagulants, compression therapy, early mobilization, treatment of the underlying cause, and prevention of recurrence. I am also detailing complications like pulmonary embolism and recurrent DVT.\n\n\n**Defining Pulmonary Embolism**\n\nI am now detailing pulmonary embolism, focusing on its etiology as a DVT clot obstruction. I'm noting the indications for treatment, like confirmed PE and preventing clot formation. I've also detailed the contraindications, which depend on the selected treatment, and potential risk factors, like surgery. I am also planning on listing the types, and required investigations. Finally, I will describe management strategies and related complications.\n\n\n**Defining Kidney Transplant Details**\n\nI am now detailing the comprehensive information on kidney transplants, including definitions, etiologies, indications, contraindications, and risk factors, using a structured approach. I've noted that end-stage kidney failure is the main indication and diabetes is a frequent cause. I'm focusing on providing details about the types of transplants: living donor and deceased donor, and I'm describing the surgical steps, like donor kidney removal, implantation, vascular connections, and ureter anastomosis.\n\n\n**Defining Transplant Outcomes**\n\nI am now detailing the complications associated with kidney transplants, specifically focusing on acute and chronic rejection, infections, bleeding, vascular thrombosis, urinary leakage, and graft failure. The section also covers investigations and the management of kidney transplants. The format follows that of the previous entries. I am now proceeding to discuss portal hypertension management in a similar structure, beginning with definitions, etiologies, indications, and contraindications.\n\n\n**Analyzing Portal Hypertension**\n\nI am now analyzing portal hypertension, detailing its risk factors like chronic alcohol use and cirrhosis. I'm noting the various management types: medical, endoscopic, TIPS, surgical shunts, and transplantation, and I'm further clarifying the investigations and available procedures. The format is consistent, moving towards a comprehensive overview.\n\n\n**Defining Esophageal Procedures**\n\nI'm now focusing on esophageal surgery for dysphagia. I've defined dysphagia, listed the typical etiologies like cancer and achalasia, and outlined the indications for surgical intervention, which include severe achalasia and esophageal obstruction. Also, I've listed contraindications like severe medical illness and types such as Heller myotomy and esophagectomy. I am also detailing the risk factors such as smoking and GERD.\n\n\n**Defining Esophageal Reconstruction**\n\nI'm now detailing esophageal reconstruction following removal, describing the surgical procedure for Heller myotomy. I'm noting the investigations like endoscopy and the management which is dictated by the underlying cause. Then, I'm providing an overview for the complications such as perforation and GERD. I also am expanding on the quick revision section, moving onto more surgical definitions.\n\n\n**Defining Surgical Outcomes**\n\nI'm now defining the structure for surgical complications like pulmonary embolism kidney transplant outcomes portal hypertension effects esophageal surgery results. I am listing complications for each to complete the surgical revision. I am using the defined format.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":9,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:05:45Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nAbsolutely. Here are Thoracic Surgeries in the same order, using simple, exam-oriented language.\n\nTHORACIC SURGERIES\n\n1. Thoracoplasty\n\nDefinition\n\nThoracoplasty is a surgical procedure in which part of the ribs is removed to collapse the chest wall over a diseased part of the lung, helping the lung cavity to close.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\t•\tOld pulmonary tuberculosis with persistent cavity\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\t•\tBronchopleural fistula\n\t•\tPersistent infected pleural cavity\n\t•\tDestroyed lung when other procedures are unsuitable\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere respiratory insufficiency\n\t•\tExtensive bilateral lung disease\n\t•\tPatient unfit for major surgery\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChronic pulmonary TB\n\t•\tPoor nutrition\n\t•\tChronic infection\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic surgery\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tComplete thoracoplasty – extensive rib removal.\n\t2.\tPartial thoracoplasty – only selected ribs are removed.\n\t3.\tApical thoracoplasty – mainly upper ribs are removed to collapse the apical cavity.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tIncision is made over the chest wall.\n\t•\tSelected ribs are exposed.\n\t•\tPortions of ribs are removed.\n\t•\tChest wall collapses inward.\n\t•\tThe underlying cavity is compressed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPulmonary function tests\n\t•\tSputum examination\n\t•\tBlood investigations\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnti-tubercular treatment when indicated\n\t•\tAntibiotics for infection\n\t•\tPain management\n\t•\tRespiratory physiotherapy\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tGradual mobilization\n\nComplications\n\t•\tChest deformity\n\t•\tReduced chest expansion\n\t•\tRestrictive respiratory impairment\n\t•\tPain\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tPoor shoulder movement\n\n⸻\n\n2. Pulmonary Resection Surgery\n\nDefinition\n\nPulmonary resection is surgery in which a diseased portion of the lung is removed.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\t•\tLung cancer\n\t•\tBronchiectasis\n\t•\tLung abscess\n\t•\tLocalized tuberculosis\n\t•\tPulmonary cysts\n\t•\tLocalized destroyed lung\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tVery poor lung function\n\t•\tExtensive bilateral disease\n\t•\tSevere cardiac disease\n\t•\tPatient unable to tolerate anesthesia/surgery\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tChronic lung disease\n\t•\tTB\n\t•\tRecurrent infections\n\t•\tPoor nutritional status\n\nTypes\n\t•\tWedge resection – small wedge-shaped portion removed.\n\t•\tSegmentectomy – one anatomical segment removed.\n\t•\tLobectomy – one lobe removed.\n\t•\tPneumonectomy – entire lung removed.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tChest is opened or minimally invasive access is used.\n\t•\tDiseased lung portion is identified.\n\t•\tBlood vessels and bronchus are divided and secured.\n\t•\tDiseased tissue is removed.\n\t•\tChest drain is usually placed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPulmonary function tests\n\t•\tBronchoscopy\n\t•\tABG\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tOxygen when required\n\t•\tChest physiotherapy\n\t•\tDeep breathing exercises\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tChest drain management\n\nComplications\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tAtelectasis\n\t•\tPneumonia\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\n⸻\n\n3. Thoracotomy\n\nDefinition\n\nThoracotomy is a surgical incision made through the chest wall to gain access to the lungs, pleura, heart, or other thoracic structures.\n\nImportant: Thoracotomy is an approach/incision, not necessarily removal of lung tissue.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tLung surgery\n\t•\tEsophageal surgery\n\t•\tPleural surgery\n\t•\tChest trauma\n\t•\tTumor removal\n\t•\tDecortication\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tPatient unable to tolerate major surgery\n\t•\tSevere uncontrolled medical illness\n\t•\tSevere respiratory insufficiency\n\nRisk Factors\n\nDepend on the underlying disease:\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tCOPD\n\t•\tPoor lung function\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tMalignancy\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tPosterolateral thoracotomy\n\t2.\tAnterolateral thoracotomy\n\t3.\tAxillary thoracotomy\n\t4.\tMedian sternotomy – commonly used for cardiac surgery, although technically different from thoracotomy.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tPatient is positioned, usually on the side.\n\t•\tIncision is made over the chest.\n\t•\tMuscles are divided or separated.\n\t•\tRibs are spread.\n\t•\tThoracic cavity is entered.\n\t•\tRequired surgery is performed.\n\t•\tChest is closed with a drain when needed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tABG\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tChest physiotherapy\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\t•\tShoulder ROM exercises\n\nComplications\n\t•\tSevere pain\n\t•\tAtelectasis\n\t•\tPneumonia\n\t•\tWound infection\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tRespiratory complications\n\t•\tShoulder stiffness\n\n⸻\n\n4. Lobectomy\n\nDefinition\n\nLobectomy is the surgical removal of one lobe of the lung.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tLocalized lung cancer\n\t•\tBronchiectasis\n\t•\tLung abscess\n\t•\tTuberculosis\n\t•\tCongenital lung disease\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tInadequate remaining lung function\n\t•\tSevere cardiac/respiratory disease\n\t•\tExtensive disease affecting remaining lung\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tCOPD\n\t•\tPoor nutritional status\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic disease\n\t•\tOlder age\n\nTypes\n\t•\tRight upper lobectomy\n\t•\tRight middle lobectomy\n\t•\tRight lower lobectomy\n\t•\tLeft upper lobectomy\n\t•\tLeft lower lobectomy\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tChest is accessed.\n\t•\tPulmonary vessels supplying the lobe are divided.\n\t•\tLobar bronchus is divided.\n\t•\tDiseased lobe is removed.\n\t•\tChest drain is inserted.\n\t•\tIncision is closed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tBronchoscopy\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tABG\n\nManagement\n\t•\tPain relief\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tIncentive spirometry\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tChest physiotherapy\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\nComplications\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tAtelectasis\n\t•\tPneumonia\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\n⸻\n\n5. Pneumonectomy\n\nDefinition\n\nPneumonectomy is the surgical removal of an entire lung.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tCentral lung cancer\n\t•\tExtensive bronchiectasis\n\t•\tDestroyed lung\n\t•\tSevere localized TB\n\t•\tExtensive unilateral lung disease\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tPoor remaining lung function\n\t•\tSevere pulmonary hypertension\n\t•\tSevere cardiac disease\n\t•\tBilateral extensive lung disease\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tCOPD\n\t•\tPoor cardiovascular function\n\t•\tOlder age\n\t•\tPoor nutritional status\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tSimple pneumonectomy – removal of one lung.\n\t2.\tExtrapleural pneumonectomy – more extensive removal including pleural structures; used in selected conditions such as some mesotheliomas.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tThoracic cavity is opened.\n\t•\tPulmonary artery and veins are divided.\n\t•\tMain bronchus is divided and closed.\n\t•\tEntire lung is removed.\n\t•\tChest is closed with appropriate drainage/management.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tEchocardiography\n\t•\tABG\n\t•\tBronchoscopy\n\nManagement\n\t•\tOxygen as required\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tChest physiotherapy\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tMonitoring of fluid balance\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\t•\tArrhythmias\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tPost-pneumonectomy syndrome\n\t•\tCardiac complications\n\n⸻\n\n6. Decortication\n\nDefinition\n\nDecortication is a surgical procedure in which a thick fibrous layer covering the lung is removed so that the lung can expand again.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\t•\tOrganized hemothorax\n\t•\tFibrothorax\n\t•\tChronic pleural infection\n\t•\tTrapped lung\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tPatient unfit for surgery\n\t•\tSevere irreversible lung disease\n\t•\tUncontrolled systemic illness\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\t•\tTuberculosis\n\t•\tPneumonia\n\t•\tHemothorax\n\t•\tPrevious pleural infection\n\nTypes\n\t•\tOpen decortication\n\t•\tVATS decortication – video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tChest is entered.\n\t•\tThick fibrous pleural layer is identified.\n\t•\tFibrous peel is carefully removed.\n\t•\tLung is allowed to re-expand.\n\t•\tChest drains are inserted.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPleural fluid analysis\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\t•\tPulmonary function tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAntibiotics\n\t•\tChest drainage\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tIncentive spirometry\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\nComplications\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tPersistent trapped lung\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\t•\tPain\n\n⸻\n\n7. Surgery for Pneumothorax\n\nDefinition\n\nPneumothorax is the presence of air in the pleural space causing partial or complete collapse of the lung.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tSpontaneous rupture of lung bleb\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tMechanical ventilation\n\t•\tLung disease\n\t•\tIatrogenic causes\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tRecurrent pneumothorax\n\t•\tPersistent air leak\n\t•\tBilateral pneumothorax in selected cases\n\t•\tFirst episode in selected high-risk situations\n\t•\tFailure of conservative treatment\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSurgery may be avoided when pneumothorax is small and stable and resolves with conservative management.\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tCOPD\n\t•\tPrevious pneumothorax\n\t•\tMechanical ventilation\n\t•\tLung disease\n\nTypes\n\t•\tPrimary spontaneous\n\t•\tSecondary spontaneous\n\t•\tTraumatic\n\t•\tIatrogenic\n\t•\tTension pneumothorax\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nCommon procedures include:\n\t•\tVATS\n\t•\tRemoval/stapling of leaking blebs or bullae\n\t•\tPleurodesis to prevent recurrence\n\t•\tSometimes pleurectomy\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPulse oximetry\n\t•\tABG when required\n\nManagement\n\t•\tObservation for selected small stable cases\n\t•\tOxygen when appropriate\n\t•\tNeedle decompression for tension pneumothorax\n\t•\tIntercostal chest tube\n\t•\tSurgery for recurrent/persistent cases\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRecurrence\n\t•\tPersistent air leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\t•\tTension pneumothorax\n\n⸻\n\n8. Surgery for Hydropneumothorax\n\nDefinition\n\nHydropneumothorax is the presence of both air and fluid in the pleural cavity.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tTrauma\n\t•\tTB\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tRuptured lung abscess\n\t•\tEsophageal rupture\n\t•\tComplication of procedures\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tLarge collection\n\t•\tPersistent air/fluid leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tTrapped lung\n\t•\tFailure of chest tube drainage\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSmall, stable collection responding to conservative treatment\n\t•\tPatient unfit for surgery\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tTB\n\t•\tChest trauma\n\t•\tPleural infection\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic procedure\n\nTypes\n\t•\tSimple hydropneumothorax\n\t•\tInfected hydropneumothorax\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tIntercostal chest tube is inserted to drain fluid and air.\n\t•\tVATS may be performed when drainage is inadequate.\n\t•\tDecortication or repair of the underlying cause may be required.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPleural fluid analysis\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\t•\tCulture\n\nManagement\n\t•\tChest drainage\n\t•\tAntibiotics if infection is present\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying disease\n\t•\tSurgery if persistent/complicated\n\nComplications\n\t•\tEmpyema\n\t•\tFibrothorax\n\t•\tPersistent air leak\n\t•\tSepsis\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\n⸻\n\n9. Surgery for Empyema\n\nDefinition\n\nEmpyema is the collection of pus/infected fluid in the pleural cavity.\n\nEtiology\n\t•\tBacterial pneumonia\n\t•\tTB\n\t•\tLung abscess\n\t•\tChest surgery\n\t•\tChest trauma\n\nIndications for Surgery\n\t•\tLoculated empyema\n\t•\tThick pleural peel\n\t•\tFailure of antibiotics and drainage\n\t•\tTrapped lung\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tPatient unable to tolerate surgery\n\t•\tVery small uncomplicated collection responding to treatment\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tPneumonia\n\t•\tTB\n\t•\tDiabetes\n\t•\tImmunosuppression\n\t•\tChest trauma\n\t•\tPrevious surgery\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tAcute empyema\n\t2.\tChronic/organized empyema\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nDepending on stage:\n\t•\tChest tube drainage\n\t•\tVATS drainage\n\t•\tBreak loculations\n\t•\tDecortication for organized empyema\n\t•\tOpen drainage in selected chronic cases\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPleural fluid analysis\n\t•\tGram stain and culture\n\t•\tCBC\n\t•\tCRP/ESR\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAntibiotics\n\t•\tChest drainage\n\t•\tNutritional support\n\t•\tRespiratory physiotherapy\n\t•\tVATS or decortication when required\n\nComplications\n\t•\tSepsis\n\t•\tFibrothorax\n\t•\tTrapped lung\n\t•\tBronchopleural fistula\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\n⸻\n\n10. Pulmonary Tuberculosis Surgery\n\nDefinition\n\nPulmonary TB surgery refers to surgical removal or drainage of localized, damaged, or complicated lung tissue due to tuberculosis, usually when medical treatment alone is insufficient.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tLocalized destroyed lung\n\t•\tPersistent cavity\n\t•\tMassive hemoptysis from localized disease\n\t•\tBronchiectasis due to TB\n\t•\tDrug-resistant localized disease in selected cases\n\t•\tPersistent complications despite adequate treatment\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tExtensive bilateral disease\n\t•\tPoor respiratory reserve\n\t•\tUnfit patient\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled systemic disease\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tPoor nutrition\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tImmunosuppression\n\t•\tDelayed diagnosis\n\t•\tDrug-resistant TB\n\nTypes\n\t•\tSegmentectomy\n\t•\tLobectomy\n\t•\tPneumonectomy\n\t•\tThoracoplasty\n\t•\tCavity procedures in selected cases\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\nDepends on disease:\n\t•\tDiseased segment/lobe may be removed.\n\t•\tIn severely destroyed unilateral lung, pneumonectomy may be considered.\n\t•\tThoracoplasty may be used in selected chronic cavities.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tSputum examination\n\t•\tGeneXpert/NAAT\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tBronchoscopy\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnti-tubercular therapy is the main treatment.\n\t•\tSurgery is used only in selected complicated cases.\n\t•\tNutritional support\n\t•\tRespiratory physiotherapy\n\t•\tExercise rehabilitation\n\nComplications\n\t•\tHemoptysis\n\t•\tBronchopleural fistula\n\t•\tEmpyema\n\t•\tRespiratory failure\n\t•\tPostoperative infection\n\t•\tRecurrence\n\n⸻\n\n11. Lung Transplant\n\nDefinition\n\nLung transplantation is a surgery in which a diseased lung or lungs are replaced by healthy donor lung tissue.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tEnd-stage COPD\n\t•\tCystic fibrosis\n\t•\tInterstitial lung disease\n\t•\tPulmonary hypertension\n\t•\tOther severe irreversible lung diseases\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tActive untreated infection\n\t•\tActive malignancy\n\t•\tSevere irreversible dysfunction of another major organ\n\t•\tInability to tolerate transplantation or long-term immunosuppression\n\nRisk Factors for Poor Outcome\n\t•\tSevere infection\n\t•\tPoor nutritional status\n\t•\tRenal dysfunction\n\t•\tCardiovascular disease\n\t•\tPoor adherence to treatment\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tSingle-lung transplant\n\t2.\tDouble/bilateral lung transplant\n\t3.\tCombined heart-lung transplant\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tDiseased lung is removed.\n\t•\tDonor lung is placed.\n\t•\tPulmonary artery and pulmonary veins are connected.\n\t•\tBronchus is connected.\n\t•\tBlood flow and ventilation are checked.\n\t•\tChest is closed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\t•\tECG\n\t•\tEchocardiography\n\t•\tInfection screening\n\t•\tTissue compatibility assessment\n\nManagement\n\t•\tLifelong immunosuppressive medication\n\t•\tInfection prevention\n\t•\tRegular lung-function monitoring\n\t•\tPulmonary rehabilitation\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tExercise training\n\nComplications\n\t•\tRejection\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tAirway complications\n\t•\tPrimary graft dysfunction\n\t•\tChronic lung allograft dysfunction\n\n⸻\n\n12. Pleurectomy\n\nDefinition\n\nPleurectomy is the surgical removal of part or all of the pleura, the membrane surrounding the lungs.\n\nIndications\n\t•\tRecurrent pneumothorax\n\t•\tPleural tumors\n\t•\tSome cases of malignant pleural disease\n\t•\tChronic pleural infection in selected cases\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere respiratory/cardiac disease\n\t•\tPatient unfit for surgery\n\t•\tExtensive disease where surgery is not beneficial\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tRecurrent pneumothorax\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tPleural malignancy\n\t•\tChronic pleural infection\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic disease\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tPartial pleurectomy – part of pleura is removed.\n\t2.\tTotal pleurectomy – extensive removal of pleura.\n\t3.\tPleurectomy/decortication – pleura and fibrous peel are removed in selected diseases.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tChest is accessed, often using VATS or open thoracotomy.\n\t•\tDiseased pleura is identified.\n\t•\tPleura is carefully separated and removed.\n\t•\tChest drains are placed.\n\t•\tChest is closed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPleural fluid analysis\n\t•\tPleural biopsy\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tChest drainage\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tIncentive spirometry\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying disease\n\nComplications\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tPersistent pain\n\t•\tRespiratory complications\n\t•\tReduced lung expansion\n\n⸻\n\n⭐ Quick Revision\n\t1.\tThoracoplasty → removal of ribs to collapse chest wall/cavity\n\t2.\tPulmonary resection → removal of diseased lung tissue\n\t3.\tThoracotomy → surgical opening of chest\n\t4.\tLobectomy → removal of one lung lobe\n\t5.\tPneumonectomy → removal of entire lung\n\t6.\tDecortication → removal of fibrous pleural peel\n\t7.\tPneumothorax surgery → treat persistent/recurrent air leak\n\t8.\tHydropneumothorax surgery → drain air + fluid and treat cause\n\t9.\tEmpyema surgery → drain pus ± decortication\n\t10.\tTB surgery → remove/manage localized destroyed or complicated lung disease\n\t11.\tLung transplant → replacement of diseased lung(s)\n\t12.\tPleurectomy → removal of pleura\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-10T19:35:45+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
