urns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n3. CARDIAC SURGERIES Easy-language study notes | Based on the supplied Cardiac Surgeries / Bailey & Love pages 823–849 These notes simplify the supplied source for exam revision. Where the requested heading (especially contraindications, heart transplant, or physiotherapy) is not specifically detailed in the supplied pages, it is clearly treated as a general study addition rather than presented as source text. 1. OPEN-HEART SURGERY Introduction / Definition Open-heart surgery is an operation in which the chest is opened so the surgeon can directly operate on the heart or great vessels. The heart is commonly approached through a median sternotomy. Types • On-pump open-heart surgery – uses cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB). • Off-pump surgery – selected operations, such as some CABG procedures, are performed on the beating heart. Indications • CABG for suitable coronary artery disease. • Congenital heart defects requiring repair or palliation. • Aortic aneurysm/dissection surgery. • Selected valve \n<truncated 19040 bytes>\n4. Burns & Plastic Surgery — S. Das\n\nBPT Exam Notes | Easy language, moderately explained\n\nNote: These are simplified, exam-oriented notes based on standard surgical concepts covered in S. Das. They are paraphrased rather than copied verbatim.\n\n⸻\n\n1. BURNS\n\nDefinition\n\nA burn is an injury to the skin and sometimes deeper tissues caused by heat, chemicals, electricity, radiation or friction.\n\nEtiology\n\n* Thermal: flame, hot liquid, steam, hot objects.\n* Chemical: acids and alkalis.\n* Electrical: electrical current.\n* Radiation: sunlight, radiotherapy, etc.\n* Friction: skin injury due to rubbing.\n\nIndications for Hospital/Surgical Management\n\n* Deep or extensive burns.\n* Burns involving face, hands, feet, genitalia, major joints.\n* Circumferential burns.\n* Electrical or chemical burns.\n* Inhalation injury.\n* Burns requiring skin grafting.\n\nContraindications\n\nThere are no absolute contraindications to emergency burn treatment.\nDefinitive surgery may be delayed if the patient is haemodynamically unstable or m\n<truncated 48560 bytes>\n5. Absolutely. Here are Thoracic Surgeries in the same order, using simple, exam-oriented language.\n\nTHORACIC SURGERIES\n\n1. Thoracoplasty\n\nDefinition\n\nThoracoplasty is a surgical procedure in which part of the ribs is removed to collapse the chest wall over a diseased part of the lung, helping the lung cavity to close.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\t•\tOld pulmonary tuberculosis with persistent cavity\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\t•\tBronchopleural fistula\n\t•\tPersistent infected pleural cavity\n\t•\tDestroyed lung when other procedures are unsuitable\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere respiratory insufficiency\n\t•\tExtensive bilateral lung disease\n\t•\tPatient unfit for major surgery\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChronic pulmonary TB\n\t•\tPoor nutrition\n\t•\tChronic infection\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic surgery\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tComplete thoracoplasty – extensive rib removal.\n\t2.\tPartial thoracoplasty – only selected ribs are removed.\n\t3.\tApical thoracoplasty – mainly upper ribs are removed to\n<truncated 16699 bytes>\n\n# Previous Session Summary:\n1. Outstanding User Requests\n- Format the \"Thoracic Surgeries\" text provided in the user's most recent message according to the strict, 